{"word": "sushi", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "sushi", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsuːʃi/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsuʃi/", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsʉʃe/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsʉʃɪ/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈsʉʃi/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}], "forms": [{"word": "sushis", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "sushi", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A Japanese dish made of small portions of sticky white rice flavored with vinegar, usually wrapped in seaweed and filled or topped with fish, vegetables or meat.", "tags": ["uncountable", "usually"], "examples": ["For the vegetarians, she served cucumber sushi.", "He went to a Japanese restaurant and ordered sushi and noodles."], "quotes": [{"text": "Suzuributa.—Mashed fish, eggs, 'sushi' of shrimps, plum cake, black mushroom, plum, and finely cut orange.", "reference": "1879 October 4, W. H. Patterson, “Japanese Cookery”, in Notes and Queries, volume s5-XII, number 301, London: John Francis, →DOI, page 263:"}, {"text": "Not long ago a newspaper reporter came to interview me on the subject of unusual foods, and I described to him the persimmon-leaf sushi made by the people who live deep in the mountains of Yoshino—and which I shall take the opportunity to introduce to you here.", "reference": "1977 [1933], 谷崎潤一郎 [Jun'ichirō Tanizaki], translated by Thomas J. Harper and Edward G. Seidensticker, In Praise of Shadows, Sedgwick, ME: Leete's Island Books, translation of 陰翳礼讃 [In'ei Raisan] (in Japanese), →ISBN, page 40:"}, {"text": "I lived in New York all my life. We used to have Chinese restaurants, ltalian restaurants. Now you have these sushi restaurants. Everyone goes for sushi. Sushi—I hate the stuff. Although, I tell you, I had some the other day. I took it home, I cooked it, it wasn't bad. It tasted like fish.", "reference": "1985, Leora Barish, Desperately Seeking Susan, spoken by Taxi Driver:"}, {"text": "To appreciate sushi, you have to forget the tastes and textures that come with Western cuisine. At first glance (or should I say taste?), a sushi of flounder will not be that different from a sushi of sea bream. But, for the Japanese, these two sushi are as different as roast beef and roast pork are for a Westerner.", "reference": "2004, Chihiro Mazui, Kazuko Masui, “Introduction”, in Sushi Secrets, London: Hachette Illustrated, →ISBN, page 12:"}, {"text": "As Trevor Corson, author of “The Story of Sushi” explains, in Japan, sushi is typically eaten at a bar, where it’s customary for the customer to chat with the chef, who can recommend what’s in season. The short window during which the fish is fresh also makes it more likely to be tastier in Japan.", "reference": "2023 June 15, Rebecca Wallwork, “‘You had to be there’: Foods that taste better on home turf”, in CNN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "sq", "name": "Albanian"}, "word": "sushi"}, {"language": {"code": "am", "name": "Amharic"}, "word": "ሱሺ"}, {"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "سُوشِي"}, {"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "սուշի"}, {"language": {"code": "az", "name": "Azerbaijani"}, "word": "suşi"}, {"language": {"code": "eu", "name": "Basque"}, "word": "sushi"}, {"language": {"code": "be", "name": "Belarusian"}, "word": "су́шы"}, {"language": {"code": "bn", "name": "Bengali"}, "word": "সুশি"}, {"language": {"code": "br", "name": "Breton"}, "word": "sushi"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "су́ши"}, {"language": {"code": "my", "name": "Burmese"}, "word": "ဆူရှီ"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "sushi"}, {"language": {"code": "yue", "name": "Chinese Cantonese"